          "urpx:description": "This filing carries the full Export Compensation Rate surface, 4,608 prices covering twelve months by twenty-four hours by weekday and weekend for each of the 2023 through 2026 interconnection vintages, split into the Generation and Delivery components that Sheet 11 step 6 sums into the customer-facing rate. Three things the published schedule states are not represented. The Sheet 11 ACC Plus adder is carried for the 2025 vintage only, at $0.01320 per kWh residential and $0.05400 per kWh low income; the 2023, 2024, 2026 and 2027 rows of the same table ($0.02200 and $0.09000, $0.01760 and $0.07200, $0.00880 and $0.03600, $0.00440 and $0.01800) are not, and neither are the four exclusions Sheets 11 and 12 attach to the adder, for customers transitioning from legacy NEM or NEM2 at the end of their legacy period, customers who bought a building with an existing system, non-residential customers, and customers required to install solar by building code. Special Condition 5 Net Surplus Compensation, paid at true-up on generation exceeding consumption over the customer's Relevant Period at a rolling twelve-month average of PG&E's default load aggregation point price between 7 a.m. and 5 p.m., together with its renewable attribute adder and the Special Condition 2.h energy true-up, which carries unused generation and delivery credits into the next Relevant Period, together with the Special Condition 2.e rule that those credits are forfeited at the last true-up once the customer is no longer served under the tariff; these settle over a year rather than a billing period, and the filing prices only the per-hour export credit. The Special Condition 2.d through 2.f rules on what an export credit may offset: generation export credits reduce only volumetric generation charges and delivery export credits only volumetric delivery charges, and four non-bypassable charges (Public Purpose Program, Nuclear Decommissioning Charge, Competition Transition Charge and Wildfire Fund Charge) may not be reduced by export credits at all, though ACC Plus credits may offset any charge. Scope is the residential NBT-S sub-schedule taking Schedule E-ELEC as its otherwise applicable schedule, so the Sheet 13 sub-schedules NBT-EXP, NBT-EXP-OTR, NBT-MT, NBT-PS and NBTA are absent, as are Special Condition 4 multiple tariff facilities, Special Condition 6 load aggregation, Special Condition 8 paired storage, Special Condition 9 disaster provisions and Special Condition 10 prevailing wage, which conditions a project's eligibility to interconnect rather than what its exports are worth.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-07T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
